* Sun Sep 13 2026 nicolataibi <nicola.taibi.1967@gmail.com> - 2026.09.13.01
  - Security (Server): Remove the master key from the GDIS boot banner. The
  banner now displays only a short, non-reversible fingerprint (first four bytes
  of the SHA-256 digest of the key material), so the most sensitive secret of
  the system is no longer written to consoles or logs.
  - Security (Server/Client): Implement genuine galaxy-state integrity
  verification. The server derives a stable verification key from the master
  key, delivers it to the client during the handshake (bound to the negotiated
  session key), and re-signs the galaxy state after every simulation tick and
  before every full synchronization (two-level HMAC-SHA256 over the frame ID,
  the galaxy cube and the scan cube). The client recomputes the signature
  locally, cross-checks the server identity digest, and displays the "VERIFIED
  (HMAC-SHA256)" badge only when the signature actually matches; on mismatch a
  prominent warning is issued and the integrity flag is cleared in the shared
  state. The badge is no longer a display of a protocol flag.
  - Security (Server/Client): Replace the unsalted password hash HMAC-
  SHA256(master, password) with a per-captain salted scheme. The client probes
  with a zero salt; the server replies with status 5 carrying the stored salt
  (or zeros for new accounts, in which case the client generates its own random
  16-byte salt); the verified hash becomes HMAC-SHA256(master, name, salt,
  password), so identical access codes are no longer linkable across accounts.
  Legacy unsalted identity records remain verifiable and are transparently
  migrated to the salted scheme on first successful login. Note: the master key
  remains the root of the password hierarchy; a per-session KDF or SRP-style
  proof is future work.
  - Security (Server/Client): Enforce strict captain-name sanitization
  (characters [A-Za-z0-9_-], maximum 32 characters) on both sides before any
  filesystem use, rejecting path-traversal names such as "x/../captains" and
  closing the offending connection. Rejects and profile deletion now release the
  reserved client slot, so a hostile client can no longer exhaust server
  capacity through repeated rejected handshakes (denial of service).
  - Security (Server): The zztop command (PERMANENT PROFILE DELETION) now also
  removes the on-disk profile: identity hash, identity salt, all frequency keys
  and the profile directory. A deleted commander can no longer log back in.
  - Security (Client): Clamp the network-supplied message length before reading
  the payload (an out-of-range length aborts the listener instead of overflowing
  the 64 KB text buffer) and move the 64 KB decryption scratch buffer from the
  listener-thread stack to the heap.
  - Security (Server/Client): Identity and frequency-key files under captains/
  are now created with 0600 permissions, and pre-existing files are tightened to
  0600 on access, removing reliance on the process umask.
  - Security (Protocol): The packet dispatcher now always consumes COMMAND and
  MESSAGE payloads; commands or messages arriving while a session is still
  synchronizing are dropped cleanly instead of desynchronizing the socket
  stream, and unsupported packet types cause the connection to be closed (fail
  closed).
  - Security (Docs): Remove the hardcoded shared secret from HOWTO.txt. The
  document now instructs generating a random 256-bit key per deployment (openssl
  rand -hex 32) and reusing the same value on server and clients.
  - Security (UI/Docs): Relabel the post-quantum frequency slots (indices
  12-14). They are currently experimental aliases implemented as AES-256-GCM and
  are no longer advertised as "Quantum Ready" or quantum-resistant in the client
  banner, the diagnostics viewer, the 3D view, in-game computer messages, or the
  documentation; an explicit implementation note has been added to README.md and
  README_it.md.

